Build plan

Phase 1: Data and logic (no visuals yet)

Decide your data shape. You need two things:
a list of categories, each with a name and a monthly budget
a list of transactions, each with an amount, a date and a category
Save and load. Write two small functions that store this data in localStorage and read it back, so nothing disappears when you refresh. Start with some hard-coded test categories and transactions.
Write the month logic. For a given month, work out the transactions that belong to it, the total spent per category, and the remaining budget per category.
Write the week logic. This is the tricky part, so do it separately and test it in the browser console:
split the month into weeks and divide each category’s budget equally
walk through the weeks in order, adding the leftover (or subtracting the overspend) from the previous week
decide how to handle weeks that straddle two months (for example, assign each week to the month where it starts)

Phase 2: Main screen

Build the page skeleton. Add a header with the month label and the view toggle (weekly/monthly), a scrollable category list, and a placeholder for the + button.
Render the category rows. Loop over your categories and draw one row each, with the name, the numbers (“42 / 100 €”) and a progress bar. The bar’s width is spent ÷ budget, capped at 100%.
Add color states. Make the bar change color as it nears or exceeds the limit (for example green, then orange, then red).
Connect the view toggle. Switching between monthly and weekly re-runs the render using the matching logic from steps 3 and 4. In weekly mode you’ll also need a way to choose which week you’re looking at.

Phase 3: Adding entries

Build the + button and the entry screen. Make a floating button at the bottom right that opens a full-screen overlay, hidden by default.
Build the form. Use a big amount input at the top, then a date input defaulting to today and a category dropdown at the bottom. Add a save button and a close button.
Connect the form to your data. On save, validate the input, create a transaction object, add it to the list, save to localStorage, close the overlay and re-render the main screen. The progress bars should now update.

Phase 4: Month navigation

Keep a “currently selected month” variable. Everything in steps 3 to 8 should read from it instead of always using today.
Make the month label clickable. Clicking it opens a picker (a simple list of months works) and selecting one updates the variable and re-renders.

Phase 5: Category detail view

Build the detail screen. Clicking a category row opens a screen showing that category’s transactions for the selected month, sorted by date, with a back button.
Add statistics. Good first candidates are average per transaction, the largest transaction, the daily average, and a projection of where you’ll land by month end.
Add edit and delete for transactions. You will need this the first time you mistype an amount.

Phase 6: Polish and deployment

Make it mobile-friendly. Check tap targets, the number keypad on the amount input (inputmode="decimal") and scrolling.
Add a way to manage categories, meaning add, rename and change a budget. You can postpone this by hard-coding categories until the rest works.
Push to GitHub Pages and test on your phone.
Back up your data. Since localStorage lives in one browser, add an export/import button so you can’t lose everything by clearing your browser data.